---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");

const questions = [
  {
    n: "01",
    q: "In what ways do current observation networks persist climate uncertainty that limit actionable decision windows, and what are the resulting technical needs?",
  },
  {
    n: "02",
    q: "How does regional vulnerability and response capacity shape the science metrics for meaningful decision making, redefining what counts as an “extreme weather event”?",
  },
  {
    n: "03",
    q: "How does the classification of data as signal, noise, or outlier shift when scientific priorities are reweighted toward decision relevance rather than forecast improvement alone?",
  },
  {
    n: "04",
    q: "What are the decision-relevant metrics for regional planners during dynamic climate events, and how do they map to data availability?",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Project — Allegra Farrar"
  description="Adaptive Sensing Design for Advanced Satellite Monitoring and Warning of Tropical Cyclones and Environmental Hazards."
>
  <section class="proj-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">PhD thesis &middot; MIT AeroAstro &middot; Space Enabled</span>
      <h1>
        Adaptive Sensing Design for
        <span class="grad-text">Advanced Satellite Monitoring</span>
        and Warning of Tropical Cyclones and Environmental Hazards
      </h1>
      <p class="lead mt-16">
        A redesign of remote sensing mission objectives and data collection
        decisions that best serve the decision-making and resilience of regions
        consistently faced with climate extremes and dynamic weather.
      </p>
      <p class="small mt-16">
        [Placeholder — this page is scaffolded from your JPL SURP seminar; refine
        the language when you have time.]
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">the problem</span>
      <h2>The most useful hours are the ones we sample worst</h2>
      <div class="grid cols-2 mt-24">
        <div>
          <p>
            Near-surface wind speed remains one of the hardest problems in
            operational meteorology. Large-scale winds high in the atmosphere or over
            open ocean are modelled well; the speed, direction, and gusts near the
            surface — the part that determines what happens to a coastline — are not.
          </p>
          <p>
            Meanwhile the observation system is largely fixed-schedule. Invests are
            declared from satellite imagery, and if a disturbance organises beneath
            thick cloud, imagers will not resolve it immediately. Forecast models
            underestimate TC intensity and are limited by the quality of their
            initialisations. The hours in which a storm decides what it is going to
            become are exactly the hours we under-sample.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            There is a second problem layered on the first: what gets discarded.
            Onboard quality flags throw away observations under strict criteria, and
            forecast pipelines lean on heavily filtered Level 2 products. A wind
            speed estimate tells you the magnitude of forcing at a location — the raw
            delay-Doppler map tells you the <em>character</em> of the environment
            producing it, which is what matters for early action.
          </p>
          <p>
            Storm awareness time in the Caribbean averages about 24 hours. CARICOOS
            needs a minimum of five days to position equipment and prepare for
            landfall. That gap is the target.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">research questions</span>
      <h2>Four questions holding the work together</h2>
      <div class="grid cols-2 mt-24">
        {questions.map((item) => (
          <article class="card q-card">
            <span class="q-num">{item.n}</span>
            <p>{item.q}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">the approach</span>
      <h2>Inference onboard, thresholds from the ground</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Adaptive sensing is a data collection approach where a system adjusts how it
        gathers observations in real time based on changing conditions or incoming
        data. Mine has two inference layers and a decision layer, sized to run on a
        CubeSat-class platform.
      </p>

      <ol class="pipeline mt-40">
        <li class="card">
          <span class="pipe-step">Always on</span>
          <h3>SNR monitor</h3>
          <p>
            A low-cost CNN watches for changes consistent with cyclogenesis
            signatures — for CYGNSS, changes in the delay-Doppler map. Roughly 1 W
            continuous.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <span class="pipe-step">Corroborate</span>
          <h3>Constellation agreement</h3>
          <p>
            Cross-satellite SNR agreement check, so a single noisy instrument does
            not trigger the expensive path on its own.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <span class="pipe-step">Layer A</span>
          <h3>DAG-GP inference</h3>
          <p>
            Triggered, not continuous. A Bayesian network of Gaussian processes
            traces how one new observation changes the estimated environmental state
            <em>and its uncertainty</em> across coupled variables. Structure fixed a
            priori from known geophysical relationships, not learned from data.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <span class="pipe-step">Layer B</span>
          <h3>Decision model</h3>
          <p>
            An expected-loss threshold set by stakeholder cost, not forecast skill
            alone. Output is an action: continue baseline, trigger advanced sensing,
            or alert.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="small mt-24">
        Builds on the DAG-GP framework from Ben Ayton's thesis (MIT, 2022) and
        Aglietti et al. (2020). Combined onboard footprint: ~1 W continuous CNN plus
        sub-millisecond triggered DAG-GP bursts, under 6 MB memory — inside CYGNSS's
        38 W spacecraft power budget and typical CubeSat OBC memory limits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">who it answers to</span>
      <h2>Design informed by the people issuing the warnings</h2>
      <div class="grid cols-3 mt-24">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>CARICOOS</h3>
          <p>
            The Caribbean Coastal Ocean Observing System deploys and processes
            coastal in-situ observation equipment, and positions gliders in
            coordination with AOML. Five days of lead time is their working minimum.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>NWS San Juan</h3>
          <p>
            Works directly with the National Hurricane Center and issues both
            official warnings and unofficial guidance on natural hazards across the
            island.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Landslide Ready</h3>
          <p>
            Trains emergency managers on assessing data to support preparation —
            especially in the post-storm window.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-24">
        Charts of billion-dollar disasters do not capture what regional stability
        actually looks like: they miss the tropical storms, coastal flooding, and
        southern-region wildfires Puerto Rico faces consistently. Part of this work
        is building a decision-needs traceability matrix — mapping what capability
        exists, what exists but is not disseminated as an alert, and what does not
        exist in a usable form at all.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">contribution</span>
      <h2>What this adds</h2>
      <div class="grid cols-2 mt-24">
        <article class="card">
          <p>
            Contributes to real-time optimisation of remote sensing by implementing
            capabilities that detect extreme weather <em>onset</em>, not just mature
            storms.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <p>
            Addresses the cyclogenesis problem by advancing near-surface sensing
            capability during pre-genesis and onset.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <p>
            Demonstrates a design cycle driven by learned end-user needs rather than
            assumed ones.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <p>
            Translates data-processing procedures into decision needs, giving
            stronger metrics for prioritisation.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="flex gap-16 mt-40" style="flex-wrap: wrap;">
        <a class="btn primary frizzle-cursor" href={`${base}/about/`}>How I got here &rarr;</a>
        <a class="btn frizzle-cursor" href={`${base}/cv/`}>CV &amp; publications</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .proj-hero { padding-top: clamp(28px, 4.5vh, 56px); }
  .proj-hero h1 { max-width: 26ch; }

  .q-card { display: flex; gap: 14px; align-items: flex-start; }

  .q-num {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--blue);
    padding-top: 3px;
    flex: none;
  }

  .q-card p { font-size: 14.5px; margin: 0; }

  .pipeline {
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    counter-reset: none;
    position: relative;
  }

  .pipeline li { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }

  .pipe-step {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--blue);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .pipeline p { font-size: 14px; }

  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .pipeline { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .pipeline { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
